 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of active cells, but with a higher constant factor for ambiguous cases due to least-squares solving. The 2D search for simple cases is $O(1)$ per cell.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the occupancy grid, lookup tables, and the output vertex array. QEF solving requires temporary storage for matrices $A$ and $b$.
-*   **Performance:** The QEF approach is more computationally expensive than standard Marching Cubes but less expensive than full Dual Contouring. The use of QEM as an alternative can further reduce register pressure on GPUs [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Produces higher quality surfaces in ambiguous regions, reducing artifacts like "popping" or incorrect topology. Maintains watertightness.
-*   **Limitations:** More complex to implement than standard Marching Cubes. Requires careful handling of degenerate cases and numerical stability in least-squares solving.
+*   **Time Complexity**: The algorithm splits processing into two paths. The 2D search path is $O(1)$ per vertex for non-ambiguous cases. The QEF path involves solving a least squares system, which is $O(1)$ per vertex but with a higher constant factor due to matrix operations [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: Requires storage for lookup tables (`amb_chks`, `axd_tab`, etc.) and the output vertex array `val_pnts` [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance**: The QEF approach is more computationally intensive than standard Marching Cubes but provides better geometric accuracy for ambiguous cases [S1, unofficial]. The QEM variant offers a trade-off between speed and accuracy, suitable for parallel hardware where register usage is a constraint [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Handles topological ambiguities robustly; produces watertight meshes; preserves volume [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: Higher computational cost than standard Marching Cubes; complex implementation due to multiple lookup tables and search strategies [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the ODC (Octree-based Discretized Contouring) implementation by the KAIST Visual AI Group [S1, unofficial]. It builds upon the classic Marching Cubes algorithm [30-isosurface-extraction.md] by incorporating geometric optimization techniques. The Quadric Error Function (QEF) was originally introduced by Garland and Heckbert for mesh simplification [GH97], and its application to isosurface extraction was adapted to handle topological ambiguities more robustly [S1, unofficial]. The QEM variant was proposed to address performance issues on parallel hardware [S2, unofficial].
+The method originates from the ODC (Octree-based Density Compression) project by the KAIST Visual AI Group, which implements a surface extraction technique combining 2D search for efficiency and QEF for accuracy [S1, unofficial]. The QEM variant is discussed in the context of generating smooth high-quality isosurfaces for interactive modeling and visualization of complex terrains, aiming to reduce computational complexity on parallel hardware [S2, unofficial].
 
